From: PS3 Custom Toolchain
Subject: [PATCH] elf64-ppc: leave a CellOS Lv-2 ILP32 .toc out of the TOC edit

ppc64_elf_edit_toc drops TOC entries that only removed sections use, and
rewrites addis/ld pairs of in-range addresses to addis/addi so their
entries can go too.  It does all of this in 8-byte steps: the skip and
used maps are indexed by offset >> 3, the section is shuffled down 8
bytes at a time, and every reference is moved by the skip of the step it
falls in.

A CellOS Lv-2 ILP32 .toc mixes 4-byte address words (R_PPC64_ADDR32)
with 8-byte entries (R_PPC64_ADDR64) that are only 4-byte aligned, and
the compiler reads the address in an 8-byte entry with lwz from the
entry's low word, at entry + 4.  An 8-byte entry at 4 mod 8 therefore
spans two steps: its relocation marks the first step optimisable, while
its lwz reference falls in the second and does not keep the first.
When the first step's other word is a dead 4-byte entry (for example one
used only by a function --gc-sections removed), ld drops that step and
moves the lwz reference back 8 bytes, onto another entry.  The program
then loads a wrong address: an engine drew with a texture descriptor
read from an unrelated global, and the RSX faulted.

Leave a .toc out of the edit unless it is a whole number of 8-byte
ADDR64 entries at 8-byte offsets.  The test is on the section's layout
and relocations, not on the data model: a .toc of aligned ADDR64 entries
is edited as before, and any other .toc is left as the compiler wrote it.
That covers an ILP32 .toc with 4-byte words or 4-aligned 8-byte entries,
and also a .toc of any data model that carries TLS or other non-ADDR64
relocations; for those the edit's optimisation is given up, and nothing
is rewritten.

--- a/bfd/elf64-ppc.c
+++ b/bfd/elf64-ppc.c
@@ -9017,6 +9017,40 @@
 	  || discarded_section (toc))
 	continue;
 
+      /* The edit below works on 8-byte TOC entries: it marks, drops and
+	 renumbers the section in 8-byte steps (indexed by offset >> 3).
+	 A CellOS Lv-2 ILP32 .toc mixes 4-byte address words (ADDR32)
+	 with 8-byte ones that may start at a 4-byte offset, and code
+	 reads the low word of an 8-byte entry at entry + 4.  An 8-byte
+	 step there can cover parts of two entries, so dropping one could
+	 remove a live word or shift a reference onto the wrong entry.
+	 Leave any .toc that is not a whole number of aligned 8-byte
+	 ADDR64 entries as it is (whatever its data model: TLS or other
+	 non-ADDR64 relocations also keep a .toc out of the edit).  */
+      if ((toc->size & 7) != 0)
+	continue;
+      if (toc->reloc_count != 0)
+	{
+	  Elf_Internal_Rela *trel;
+	  bool whole_entries = true;
+
+	  trel = _bfd_elf_link_read_relocs (ibfd, toc, NULL, NULL,
+					    info->keep_memory);
+	  if (trel == NULL)
+	    return false;
+	  for (rel = trel; rel < trel + toc->reloc_count; ++rel)
+	    if ((rel->r_offset & 7) != 0
+		|| ELF64_R_TYPE (rel->r_info) != R_PPC64_ADDR64)
+	      {
+		whole_entries = false;
+		break;
+	      }
+	  if (elf_section_data (toc)->relocs != trel)
+	    free (trel);
+	  if (!whole_entries)
+	    continue;
+	}
+
       toc_relocs = NULL;
       local_syms = NULL;
       symtab_hdr = &elf_symtab_hdr (ibfd);
